        <responseType>Avoid</responseType>
        <urgency>Immediate</urgency>
        <severity>Severe</severity>
        <certainty>Likely</certainty>
        <eventCode>
            <valueName>SAME</valueName>
            <value>SMW</value>
        </eventCode>
        <eventCode>
            <valueName>NationalWeatherService</valueName>
            <value>MAW</value>
        </eventCode>
        <effective>2025-08-02T09:33:00-05:00</effective>
        <onset>2025-08-02T09:33:00-05:00</onset>
        <expires>2025-08-02T10:30:00-05:00</expires>
        <senderName>NWS New Orleans LA</senderName>
        <headline>Special Marine Warning issued August 2 at 9:33AM CDT until August 2 at 10:30AM CDT by NWS New Orleans LA</headline>
        <description>SMWLIX

The National Weather Service in New Orleans has issued a

* Special Marine Warning for...
Coastal Waters from Boothville LA to Southwest Pass of the
Mississippi River out 20 nm...
Coastal waters from Southwest Pass of the Mississippi River to Port
Fourchon Louisiana from 20 to 60 NM...
Coastal waters from the Southwest Pass of the Mississippi River to
Port Fourchon Louisiana out 20 NM...

* Until 1030 AM CDT.

* At 933 AM CDT, a cluster of severe thunderstorms capable of
producing waterspouts was located along a line extending from 39 nm
south of Grand Isle to 16 nm southeast of Louisiana Offshore Oil
Port to 8 nm west of Southwest Pass Of the Mississippi River,
moving east at 15 knots.

HAZARD...Waterspouts and wind gusts 34 knots or greater.

SOURCE...Radar.

IMPACT...Waterspouts can quickly form and capsize boats...damage
vessels and oil rigs...and create suddenly higher waves.
Small craft could be damaged in briefly higher winds and
suddenly higher waves.

* Locations impacted include...
Southwest Pass Of the Mississippi River.
</description>
        <instruction>Strong winds will be dangerous for boaters or anyone near the water.
Boaters should avoid waterspouts and never try to navigate near them.
Make sure all on board are wearing life jackets. Seek shelter or move
to safe harbor until hazardous weather passes.</instruction>
        <web>http://www.weather.gov</web>
